  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery shows the upper level ridge over the \nPlains with an upstream closed low spinning over the Pacific \nNorthwest, a downstream trough over Quebec, and an elongated \nshortwave just north of Lake Superior. At the sfc, high pressure \nover Hudson Bay is extending ridging south over Ontario and into the \nCWA. Despite the overcast skies, SPC mesoanalysis indicates MUCAPE \nbetween 500-1000 J/kg over the west supported by Tds in the mid 60s. \nTemps are not expected to climb much further today, peaking in the \nupper 60s to mid 70s. Isolated showers continue into tonight, \ngradually diminishing as instability aloft falls and high pressure \nbuilds in from the north. Mostly negligible rainfall amounts, but \nisolated totals of a few hundreths is forecast. Fog development is \nexpected in the west tonight courtesy of ample low level moisture \nand light winds. Particularly focused on fog near the WI/MI state \nline where patchy dense fog down to 1/4 mile is possible (20-40% \nchance) into early Saturday morning. Otherwise lows will be in the \nmid 50s to low 60s.\n\nWith high pressure and upper level ridging dominating the remainder \nof Labor Day weekend, dry weather is expected. Temperatures remain 5 \nto 10 degrees above normal with highs in the 70s to low 80s and lows \nmainly in the 50s to low 60s. Saturday night is an exception with \nnear normal lows in the mid 40s to mid 50s as drier air, light winds \nand mostly clear skies under high pressure influence support \nradiative cooling processes.\n\nPrecip chances return Monday night from the west, continuing into \nTuesday night as the upper level ridge moves east and flattens. This \ngives way to the deep western trough to lift northeast to Manitoba \nby the end of the day Tuesday, then tracking east to Quebec. A \nresulting sfc low from the northern Rockies lifts to Manitoba by \nTuesday morning, then moves across far northern Ontario to Quebec \nthrough Wednesday. Showers and storms return to the UP ahead of the \nassociated cold front, mainly on Tuesday. This does not look to \nbring much in the way of strong storms, but it does present the best \nrainfall chances as of recent. NBM probabilities of at least 1 inch \nof rain in 24 hours are between 30-50%. This system also yields \nbreezy winds. Initially expecting south winds on Tuesday ahead of \nthe cold front gusting between 15-30 mph, particulary in ideal \nsoutherly downslope flow. Behind the front, pressure rises and CAA \nsupport stronger westerly winds on Wednesday with frequent gusts of \n20-30 mph. Probabilities for wind gusts exceeding 40 mph are between \n30-60% in the Keweenaw.\n\nThere is a slight chance (15-24% chance) for additional showers late \nWednesday into Thursday with a shortwave to the north and a \nsecondary cold front pressing south. Otherwise temps return near \nnormal in the mid 60s to mid 70s late next week.",
